# The Aryan Illusion
Tamil title: ஆரிய மாயை

Praise to you, great lord of greed!

Praise to you, possessor of two tongues!

Praise to you, embodiment of intrigue!

Praise to you, leader of slaves!

Praise to you, deceitful king!

Praise to you, hard-hearted lord!

Praise to you, paragon of cruelty!

Praise to you, coward—praise, praise!

Praise to you, supreme personification of fear!

Praise to you, worker of grievous fraud!

Praise to you, sower of quarrels!

Praise to you, laughing fox!

Praise to you, master of the art of clinging!

Praise to you, possessor of exalted injustice!

Praise to you, destroyer of our people!

Praise to you, peerless ruin!

Here is your prey—praise, praise!

I have extolled you—praise, praise!

This sacred hymn of praise seems novel, does it not? Its meaning is unclear. Is it not foolish to praise people possessed of greed, deceit and other harmful qualities? Can it be wise to worship what ought to be abolished? Not even madmen call the scorpion a god, the snake the Supreme Being, the fox a lord or the tiger a holy one.

“Yet you lavish praise upon deceivers and people with two tongues, crying ‘Praise, praise!’ What is this, Bharatha?” friends will ask.

It is not I who proclaim this, gentlemen. Among our people are “good Sudras” endowed with broad minds, expansive affection, tender hearts and floods of compassion. Do they not live forever praising the Aryans? Those who know have described the sacred and auspicious qualities of the “gods upon earth” whom these devotees praise and worship. My work was merely to gather those descriptions, join them to the “praise, praise” performed by Brahmin devotees, hurriedly turn them into an *akaval*, and give it to you.

I neither praise the Aryans nor ask you to praise them. Likewise, I neither abuse them nor urge you to do so. I repeat the censure uttered by another.

Devotees who worship the agraharam every day will curse me: “Why, Bharatha, and upon what authority, do you call the gods upon earth deceitful without so much as a stumble of the tongue? Why do you agitatedly call them terrible cheats and abuse them in so many ways? You are loading yourself with sin. Sinner, you will suffer in the hell of Raurava. Go!”

I tell them: this verse I compiled was not coined as abuse. A European took the portrait many years ago; I merely publish it today. The goods are not ours, nor have we fabricated what does not exist. Read the hymn of praise once again now. Have you read it? Very well—here are several English words!

*[The English word-list in the scan is severely corrupted by OCR.]*

Now use a dictionary to see what those English words mean in Tamil. Then consider whether the hymn of praise I composed is correct, exaggerated or wrong.

Comrades, the scholar Abbé Dubois used terms of this kind to describe the Aryans—not today or yesterday, not after hearing Dr Nair's drum, and not because he had been bitten by communal controversy. Having seen with his own eyes, understood with his mind and spoken openly, he did so in 1807.

The book was published in 1807. In it, he described Brahmins in this fashion and depicted the character of the Aryan people very clearly. Yet even today many Tamils worship people of this character, call them a high caste and address them as *swami*. What a condition theirs is!

Abbé Dubois was a French priest. One hundred and thirty-six years ago, he recorded the truth he said he had discovered here. In describing Brahmin character, he used the very terms I have turned into a hymn of praise. No one can simply say, “What he wrote was wrong; he had a jaundiced outlook.” The peerless newspaper *The Hindu* reviewed Abbé Dubois's book. More than that, *The Hindu* wrote that people here remained in the same condition the priest had described.

Some will ask: “Would it not be foolish if that foreigner once happened to see some worthless Aryan and generalised from him? Every country and people contains a few scoundrels. Can an entire people therefore be described as possessing that character? Is it proper or fair?”

I say three times that it would not be proper, and if necessary I shall say six times that it would not be fair. It is foolish to take one person's bad quality and accuse the whole people to which that person belongs.

But Abbé Dubois travelled here for thirty years. He journeyed throughout the country and closely observed people's daily lives. Only after seeing what he considered the character of the Aryan people as it was did he record it in writing.

Adventurous minds may say: “Very well, Abbé Dubois was a French priest and must have written in French. Could the person who translated it into English not have twisted the rope?” The Englishman who translated Dubois's French book was H. K. Beauchamp. But the person who supervised it was the “god upon earth” C. V. Munuswami Iyer. Would he have remained silent if it contained an error?

Thus Dubois knew the Aryans well before describing them in this fashion. Because he was not caught and bewitched by the Aryan illusion, he could depict what he saw as it was. Leave the litany aside, friends, and listen to some of the ideas expressed in his writing.

“Greed is natural to every member of that Brahmin community; they therefore cannot live in the detached condition of Vedantins.”

That is Dubois's statement. The world knows well that it still contains truth, the author argues. Do we not even today see Sankaracharyas, who preach that the world is illusion, travelling in golden palanquins? Do we not see Vedanta and renunciation displayed alongside feasts and festivals? This, says the essay, is what Dubois observed even then.

Dubois writes that intrigue, deceit, double speech and ingratiating smiles are natural to them, and that their object is somehow to approach kings and obtain office. History, the author says, offers many examples.

We read that kings of the Puranic and epic age behaved towards rishis and sages with fear, devotion and loyalty; offered even their jewelled crowns; granted whatever they asked; and carried upon their heads whatever work the sages assigned with their feet. Under kings of the historical age, Sharmas, Iyers and Iyengars lived as diwans and ministers.

Who even now is Diwan of Travancore? Sir C. P. Ramaswami Iyer. In Baroda one finds Sir V. T. Krishnamachari, and in Mewar Sir T. Vijayaraghavacharya. The man who served as Chief Secretary to our provincial government and is now its adviser is S. V. Ramamurthy Iyer. A former Premier of our province was Rajagopalachari, and our provincial legal expert and Advocate-General is Sir Alladi Krishnaswami Iyer.

What Dubois said in 1807 remains, the article declares, an evident truth—not merely like a gooseberry in one's palm, but, to use a more fitting comparison, like a wound upon the hand.

Dubois writes that after approaching the kings of that age and obtaining positions, Aryans dared to commit injustice, fraud, misconduct and cruelty. He says they were adept at tying quarrels together and stirring rebellion. Otherwise, asks the author, would the Aryan mind have invented a god whose very occupation was creating conflict? Are they not the supreme devotees who worship Narada? Wherever they cast it, the consequence finally dawns upon the heads of our people.

Dubois also records that rulers did not employ Brahmins merely because they were capable. Aryans had acquired the “power” to boast of high caste before other people and keep them under control; kings therefore wanted Vedic men in office so that ordinary people would cause no nuisance.

Even today, observe how submissively members of our people behave towards them, whether a Brahmin is a schoolteacher, a temple priest, a broker for the world of salvation or a seller of snacks. The mentality that says abusing a Brahmin is a great “sin” still exists.

Yet, for all this, Dubois says they are not incomparable heroes and possess no courage, but are cowardly at heart—a company whose thighs tremble at the sight of an army. What keeps warriors subdued beneath them? Is it not that our people lie fallen under the Aryan illusion? I ask whether anyone who considers impartially what scholars such as Dubois have written about Aryan character can continue living in praise of Aryans.

> Mother, may you live long—listen to me!
> The horse from that land of lofty hills
> has a mane drooping upon its head
> like the little Brahmin boy of our town.

—*Ainkurunuru*

“Mother, look! He comes riding a horse so majestically!”

“Who is coming, girl?”

“The lord of the country surrounded by great mountains comes riding a steed.”

“Very well. What is so astonishing about that?”

“The horse comes shaking its head. It looks amusing, Mother!”

“What is amusing about that?”

“When the horse shakes its head, its clustered mane dances with it. The sight makes me laugh.”

“Why should a mane dancing on a horse's head make you laugh?”

“Why, Mother! Does it not look like the topknots on the heads of the Brahmins in our village, dancing as they walk? That is why I laughed.”

“Go away, you mischievous girl!”

The horseman travels down the street; the horse shakes its head and its mane moves. Seeing it, the friend remembers the Brahmin topknot and laughs. In ancient Tamilakam she tells the heroine what she saw and thought. The verse quoted above contains this idea. It comes from the anthology *Ainkurunuru*; it was not manufactured at Erode.

There was an age when Tamils regarded Aryans as walking figures of ridicule; an age when awareness that the Aryan people were distinct had not faded; an age that regarded Aryanism as a comic performance. Today, by contrast, the false idea flourishes that possessing the intelligence, ritual observances, religious discipline, worship, gait, dress and manner of Aryans gives Tamils virtue and excellence.

Because this poisonous cactus, made to sprout by time, has spread, destructive quarrels move among us, touching and scorching Tamil society.

Researchers have clearly stated that Aryan culture and Dravidian culture are different.

German scholars were the first to praise Aryan civilisation, language and art to the skies. When the German Max Müller undertook this work, the agraharam here danced with joy. “See how greatly Westerners esteem the Vedas and Smritis; hear how our fame has spread everywhere,” they said with swelling pride.

New researchers then arose to burn away what the essay calls the shallow knowledge of German scholars who praised the Aryan people instead of condemning their character as Dubois had done. It took time for the false assertion that Aryans first introduced civilisation into India to disappear. It took years for the world to understand that a rich civilisation had flourished here from great antiquity without Aryan assistance.

The world did not initially understand the author's claim that ancient Tamil life was ruined, courage declined, art was stained and society destabilised through association with Aryanism. It called India Aryavarta and Indian civilisation itself Aryan civilisation.

Only when Professor Sir John Marshall researched and described Dravidian characteristics, the article argues, were the scales removed from Western eyes and the stain from Western thought. Research then began into how extensively Aryanism had damaged Dravidian civilisation.

Later scholars explained that Aryans had introduced fabricated tales among Dravidians—who enjoyed natural pleasures, honoured courage and upheld virtue—and thereby cultivated cowardice. They argued that before Aryan doctrines entered, Dravidians had devoted themselves to intellectual inquiry rather than immersing themselves in doctrines invisible to the eye, beyond thought, impervious to argument and inconsistent with empirical proof.

Yet all this was treated merely as a post-mortem examination, not used as a hymn of awakening for a fallen people. Among a Dravidian society where basic education and general knowledge had not spread widely, there was no means for the findings of new research to circulate. That, says the author, is why the sound idea that Aryanism brings destruction while Dravidianism develops the people has still not entered the minds of our community.

If those accomplished in education and questions of learning, and those with abundant facilities for research, labour even a little in this field, the Dravidian people can be saved. Otherwise, just as many peoples of the world have been ruined and disappeared, the Dravidian people who once lived in worldwide renown will also perish.

Any people that loses its character, forgets its ancient greatness and seeks refuge with its enemy is walking rapidly towards the pit of destruction.

> Though enemies heap up a crore,
> calling, “Touch us and be saved,” I shall not touch.

Thus sings our poet Kanaka Subburathinam—Bharathidasan—as though he were the very embodiment of the awakening of a people. The phrase “I shall not touch” bursts from the living poet's heart, radiant with determination and passion.

“I shall not become my enemy's servile follower, even if he gives me a crore,” says the poet. A crore of rupees, sovereigns, diamonds or heaps of the nine gems—whatever you accumulate before me by the crore, I shall not become your lackey.

The poet does not join the word *crore* to any noun naming a particular thing. Why? If he specified one thing, would that not leave room for the enemy to offer a crore of something else? The enemy offers a crore of rupees and the Tamil refuses; the cunning enemy might immediately offer a crore of varahans. The poet gives no room even for such bargaining. Whatever it may be, pile up a crore and still I do not want it: that is his final declaration.

In the poet's “I shall not touch,” the meaning is not merely that he will not touch with his hand; it also implies that he will not touch it even in thought. Recite the verse once more:

> Though enemies heap up a crore,
> calling, “Touch us and be saved,” I shall not touch.

If such feeling and resolve, such a mind without the slightest thought of self-interest, such resistance to temptation and such courage existed, the enemy could not prevail even if a crore of spears were pointed straight at Tamil chests. Where do we find that resolve? From whom do we hear such heroic speech?

Instead, we see selfish people roaming Tamil Nadu saying, “What is it to us?”; trembling cowards asking, “Can we accomplish it?”; timid people saying, “Why invite needless trouble?”; and obstructive people declaring brusquely, “I do not like this.”

> My warrior-kings lived holding honour alone
> to be the good life.
> Their descendants are no cats:
> Tamil people are the equals of tigers!

On the day this heroic roar is heard in every Tamil home, on the day Tamil hearts attain this state, the Aryans will realise: “The descendants of the Chera who made Kanakavijayan carry stone have become Tamils once more; our trade will no longer prosper.” They will bare all their teeth and submit.

The author argues that Aryans have no history of fighting and subduing enemies, but only of approaching adversaries and destroying them from within. Their names are not associated with great wars. Even the wars said to occur in Puranas and epics are inventions, yet not even those tales say victory came through courage. They speak only of victory through sacrifice, strength through divine grace and an enemy defeated by magical weapons. In Dravidian histories, courage occupies first place.

The Aryan people did not fight and earn fame, the essay claims; their arrogance grew only when others lost their reason.

An intoxicated man falls when he stumbles over a stone or is struck by the wind. In the same manner, Aryans first created intoxication in Dravidian thought and then rolled the people down. The Dravidian was not defeated by Aryan courage; he became a bearer of the burden of Aryan ideas, grew weary and curled up. Those who fail to understand this subtlety do not know Tamil history.

“Gentlemen! Tamils who consider themselves intellectuals do not believe those who say the forms you now worship are ‘Aryan imagination’; they mock them. These intellectuals complain that scoundrels call everything Aryan fraud, Aryan conspiracy and fabrication.”

A buffalo does not say, “Rain indeed! What can rain do to us?” Its conduct reveals its thought. Among Tamils there are thick-skinned people, just as among four-legged animals there is the buffalo.

No number of texts illuminates the minds of simpletons who say that some people babble of Aryans and Dravidians merely through useless delusion. They readily claim that only members of the Self-Respect Movement possess an irrational hatred of Aryans. What accusation can they make against the views of professors devoted to intellectual inquiry who have no connection with the Self-Respect Movement?

When Aryans narrated their ideas and divine stories, they did not concern themselves with whether these accorded with reason or could withstand investigation. Does a master explain to a slave the reason for his mastery? When elders frighten a child with stories of a five-eyed being or a six-legged one, does the terrified child listen open-mouthed and then say, “Grandfather, I cannot believe this”?

It was during human society's childhood that divine stories were constructed about a god of thunder, a mother of lightning, rain, Mahakali and a god of fire. Stories that arose in this fashion throughout the world began to disappear when the peoples of the earth reached intellectual maturity.

Here alone, the author argues, Aryans stubbornly retain the obscenities of that age and make their livelihood by spreading the same ideas among the people. Thus absence of knowledge has been taught as wisdom; saying that what exists does not exist as Vedanta; and making people believe that what does not exist does exist as religion.

Would a man who lives by selling opium preach against intoxication?
